BUILD-DISCIPLINE candidate: CHECK THE DECLARED SET, NEVER THE INTERSECTION -- and an installer looks vestigial from inside the development loop. Two joined rows, both earned on 2026-07-29 by tearing dexter down to a bare host (realisateur 76e8662, f7fbaf5). ROW A -- a deploy/health check that compares what the repo ships against WHAT IS ALREADY INSTALLED reports a bare host as clean. schedulers bin/deploy-drift-check.sh iterates the INTERSECTION of repo bin/ and $DEPLOY_DIR (line 28, "per file in this repos bin/ that also exists in $DEPLOY_DIR"), so a utility that SHOULD be installed but is not is never examined; line 205 prints "nothing in $DEPLOY_DIR shares a name with this repos bin/ -- nothing to check" and exits 0. It catches a DANGLING symlink and never an ABSENT one. Absence read as health. The rule: iterate what the repo DECLARES, so a missing item is a finding; an intersection-based check cannot distinguish "all present" from "none installed". ROW B -- the reason the installer was gone at all is subtler and is the more valuable half. bin/scheduler DID install its own symlinks via "pacing deploy" until 2026-07-27, when a5fb620 retired it: "installed scripts are symlinks into the repo, so there is no copy to re-deploy." That rationale is TRUE. Inside a development checkout the repo IS what runs, editing it changes behavior immediately, and a cp-era deploy step really is vestigial. But "no copy to REFRESH" was read as "nothing to INSTALL", and creating the link in the first place went out with the habit it belonged to. Invisible on every host that already had the link; on a bare one it was a TOTAL dispatch outage -- the runner tick is not a job on dexter, it is the only job, so the generated crontab came out empty. The rule: an installer looks like a no-op from inside the loop that does not need it, so the only safe shape is one where RE-DERIVING IS NORMAL OPERATION rather than a separate step -- every run recomputes the installed set from the repo, so there is never a moment at which skipping it looks harmless. Corollary for review: when retiring a deploy mechanism, ask who CREATES the thing, not just who refreshes it. Note the good news too, which is why sync-crontab is the model: meta_cmd_unrunnable checked the target, named it, and OMITTED the tier rather than emitting a cron line that would fail silently every 30 minutes. Of three mechanisms only that one fired. Related: this is run 3s own thesis (a tick nobody generated) one layer down, and it is the design constraint now carried into office/bootstrap.sh and basheur.
